{
  "outcome": "resolved",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Hannah, thanks for checking with us first, and good call not replying. That email is not from us. IT will never ask for your password by email, chat, or phone, for any reason. It's a phishing attempt. Here's what to do: 1) Don't reply, click any links, open any attachments, or forward it to anyone. 2) Use the Report Phishing button in Outlook so our security team gets a copy, then delete it. I looked at your account and everything is normal: all recent sign-ins are from your usual places and times, and I found nothing suspicious. Two quick follow-ups: if the email came from a real @brightline.example address, or if you hear that coworkers got the same message, please reply and tell us so we can look into it. And if you ever do click a link or type your password somewhere by mistake, contact the help desk right away.",
  "internal_note": "User reports a phishing email posing as 'IT Support' that asked her to reply with her password. She says she did not reply. Following KB-008. Account is active with 0 failed logins. must_change_password=true and the password was last set 2026-10-09, which fits a recent account setup and may explain the 'finish setting up your account' lure. Quarantine is empty, so no similar messages were caught. Sign-in logs for the last 14 days show 9 successful sign-ins, all from New York on corporate or her usual residential IP during normal hours, with no hosting networks and no failures. No sign of compromise, so KB-029 does not apply. Nothing in KB-008's escalation criteria is met so far: only one user has reported it, and the sender domain is unknown. I asked the user to report it if it came from an internal address or if others got it. Separately: the user is NOT enrolled in Duo MFA and every sign-in shows mfa=none. I recommend a technician follow up on Duo enrollment for this payroll user.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
